# The taglines are append-only and everybody appends to the end of them, which
# is the one shape of file git is worst at merging. union keeps both lines
# instead of asking, and since a duplicate or a missing line is repaired by the
# next  tools/chronicle.sh  anyway, there is nothing here worth stopping a
# pilot mid-pull to resolve.
docs/taglines.tsv merge=union

# Generated after every commit, by everybody. Never worth a conflict: rebuild
# it and the argument is over.
docs/index.html   merge=union
docs/chronicle.css merge=union

# One page per version, same machine, same argument. A union merge of two whole
# pages is nonsense to read, but it is nonsense that still opens in a browser
# and that the next  tools/chronicle.sh  throws away - which beats stopping a
# pilot mid-pull over a file neither of them wrote.
docs/v*.html      merge=union

# The splash screen's window into the book, and the rail beside it. Written by
# the same machine on every rebuild and thrown away by the next one, exactly
# like the pages above - and left off this list until a collection that was
# supposed to be silent stopped to ask a pilot about all three.
#
# The union of two of these can be nonsense to read, and once was: what it
# cannot be is a lost intention, because nobody writes a line of either by
# hand. The nonsense has a lifetime of one commit, which is the merge, and the
# referee passes over a merge for its own reasons anyway.
docs/chronicle.js merge=union
docs/rail.js      merge=union

# The ledger, and the only generated file in the project that does not live
# under docs/ - which is precisely how it got left off this list. It is
# rewritten by  tools/tally.sh  off the history, nobody may edit it by hand
# (GR12), and duplicate keys in an object literal are legal JS that the rebuild
# replaces on the spot. So there was never a human intention in here for a
# union to lose, and never a reason for it to stop anybody.
src/game/ledger.js merge=union

# The plates and the list of them. A plate is asked for once and never asked
# for again, so two clones can only ever have painted different versions, not
# the same one differently — union is exactly right for the list, and the
# pictures themselves are binary and never touched twice.
docs/art/index.tsv merge=union
docs/art/*.jpg    binary
docs/art/*.png    binary
docs/art/*.webp   binary

# The faces, and the same argument once more with one thing added: a pilot is
# painted once and never again, so two clones can only ever have painted
# different people. Union keeps both, which is the right answer and also the
# only one — a conflict here would be two clones arguing over somebody's face,
# and neither of them is entitled to settle it. faces.js is written out of the
# list one assignment per line for exactly this reason.
docs/faces/index.tsv merge=union
docs/faces/faces.js  merge=union
docs/faces/*.jpg  binary
docs/faces/*.png  binary
docs/faces/*.webp binary

# One page per pilot, and the reason they were left off this list for a commit
# is the reason they need to be on it more than the chapters do: a chapter page
# changes when its own version changes, and a pilot page changes when anybody
# at all commits. The roster, the cross-visits, the dock and the flight meter
# are all facts about the whole history, so two branches that each landed one
# thing have each rewritten every pilot page in the book - including the pages
# of pilots who were not there. Measured before this line was written: two
# branches off the same commit, touching different files in src/, conflicted on
# all three pilot pages and on nothing else.
docs/pilot-*.html merge=union
